# Responsive UI QA

After building or changing UI, validate layouts deliberately — not only at one desktop width.

## Viewport matrix (CSS px)

| Width | Intent |
| --- | --- |
| **320** | Smallest phones — no overflow, stacked layout |
| **375–390** | Mainstream phones — nav + CTAs usable |
| **768** | Tablet — grids collapse correctly |
| **1024** | Small laptop / landscape tablet |
| **1280–1520** | Desktop — breathing space, max content width |

Also check **just below / above** each layout change (`md`, `lg`).

## Must pass

- No unintended **horizontal scroll** at 320px (WCAG reflow spirit)
- Sticky header never permanently covers section titles (`scroll-margin` / scroll helpers)
- Nav: hamburger usable on mobile; desktop links readable; drawer closes after navigate
- Touch targets comfortable (~44px where practical; WCAG minimum 24×24 with spacing)
- Long Persian titles wrap cleanly — no clipped text or broken badges
- Images / abstract visuals scale; decorative layers use `pointer-events-none`
- Forms and dialogs fit the viewport; keyboard focus remains visible
- Desktop feels spacious; mobile stays single-column and thumb-friendly

## Tailwind notes

- Mobile-first: unprefixed utilities apply to all sizes; `sm:`/`md:`/`lg:` mean **min-width**
- Prefer fluid containers and grids (`grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3`) over fixed pixel layouts
- Test RTL: start/end spacing (`ms-`/`me-`, logical properties) instead of assuming LTR `ml`/`mr`

## Reporting

When UI work finishes, briefly confirm which widths were considered and any known residual risk.
